  "id": "Compound::DB01160 - Gene::55867",
  "verbalization": "Dinoprost Tromethamine is shown to interact with Slc22a11 through a series of mechanistic pathways, primarily involving the sodium-independent organic anion transporter. The drug binds to Slco3a1 or Slco2a1, which are both involved in the transport of organic anions across cellular membranes. These transporters, in turn, are part of the broader Slc22a family, specifically participating in the transport of organic anions, and ultimately, they are linked to Slc22a11. The evidence suggests that Dinoprost Tromethamine may modulate the function of Slc22a11 by influencing the activity of its paralogous transporters. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though the strength is lower compared to the first two paths. The mechanism involves the drug's interaction with a transporter that facilitates the movement of anions, potentially affecting the transport of specific molecules across cell membranes. This pathway is consistent with the role of Slc22a11 in anion transport and may have implications for drug development targeting this gene.",
